  {
   "who": "N",
   "say": "First, the simple version. Inflation means prices go up, so the same money buys less stuff.",
   "show": {
    "k": "scene",
    "bg": "market",
    "cast": [
     {
      "id": "frau",
      "x": 0.4,
      "mood": "neutral",
      "hold": "bread"
     }
    ],
    "objects": [
     {
      "k": "sign",
      "x": 0.8,
      "s": 0.6,
      "text": "BREAD ↑"
     },
     {
      "k": "crate",
      "x": 0.12,
      "s": 0.5,
      "label": "FOOD"
     }
    ],
    "stickers": [
     {
      "text": "PRICES UP",
      "at": 1.0,
      "color": "#A8322A"
     }
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "who": "N",
   "say": "A little inflation is normal. That's why your grandparents paid way less for a candy bar than you do."
  },
  {
   "who": "N",
   "say": "Hyperinflation is inflation gone wild. One classic rule says prices rising fifty percent or more in a single month.",
   "show": {
    "k": "card",
    "label": "Hyperinflation: one classic definition",
    "big": "50% a month",
    "sub": "prices rise by half or more, every month (economist Phillip Cagan, 1956)"
   }
  },
  {
   "who": "N",
   "say": "And it almost always starts the same way. A government needs money it doesn't have, so it simply makes more.",
   "show": {
    "k": "scene",
    "bg": "office",
    "cast": [
     {
      "id": "banker",
      "x": 0.35,
      "mood": "smug",
      "pose": "present"
     }
    ],
    "objects": [
     {
